I reproduced end-to-end connectivity from your PC (pc4, 172.16.10.4) to the upstairs machine (laptop0, 172.16.20.10) and back — both directions succeed with 0% loss and ~1–2 ms RTT. This proves inter-floor reachability is currently healthy.

The inter-floor path here is Layer-2 VLAN trunks up to a Layer-3 gateway on router0. On router0, both subinterfaces for your floor and the upstairs floor are up and correctly addressed (eth0.10 = 172.16.10.1/24, eth0.20 = 172.16.20.1/24), and routes are present. On your access switch (switch1), both VLAN bridges are operational with the trunk subinterfaces up (eth0.10 and eth0.20 members of br_vlan10 and br_vlan20 respectively), so the uplink towards the distribution (mswitch1) is carrying both floors’ VLANs as expected. Your PC’s default route via 172.16.10.1 is correct.

Mechanism-wise, if the trunk or router subinterfaces were down/mis-tagged, ICMP between 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24 would fail. Since pings succeed and gateway/trunking are up, there is no active fault to localize.

Root cause: Not reproducible; the network elements joining the floors (switch1 trunk to mswitch1 and router0 VLAN interfaces) are functioning normally at this time.

Corrective fix: None required. Please retest from PC4. If this recurs, we’ll capture counters/logs on mswitch1/switch1 and ARP/route tables on router0 at the time of failure to isolate any transient issue. 
